{
  "abstract": "We study biplanar graphs near the edge cap of six times the order minus twelve. A planar separator inequality relates the layer edge deficit to the cycle rank induced by a separator. When a graph has total deficit zero, one or two, deletion of a separator leaves exactly two components, and its induced cycle rank meets the inequality with equality, we prove that each planar layer has a forced geometric interface: either one separator cycle in a triangulation or one separator path in a near-triangulation with a single quadrilateral. Counting the two triangulated disks yields an exact allocation identity for the separator edges and vertices. It gives a finite necessary biplanarity test even when the two layer interfaces share vertices. We supply complete proofs, an enumerator, and explicit planar positive controls at all three deficits. Two new 21-vertex graphs, with 114 and 113 edges respectively, fail the allocation test by a single edge that cannot belong to either layer. Both have clique number seven. For each graph, exhaustive checks of all induced subsets and all vertex separators find no violation of the usual density or basic separator bounds. Thus the allocation condition detects a structural obstruction beyond those elementary tests. The result is useful for rejecting dense candidate graphs and deriving sound finite-search cuts, but it does not establish a chromatic lower bound or resolve the unrestricted Earth--Moon problem.",
